WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:06.930
Despite being among the most remarkable and
ambitious shows in TV history, HBO unceremoniously

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.520
canceled Deadwood after just three seasons.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.340
In 2019 13 years later the show finally returned
with a wrap-up film.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.860
So just why did HBO get rid of Deadwood in
the first place?

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:23.240
The business of making television is somewhat
separate from the business of broadcasting

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:23.740
it.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.100
While many networks are part of entertainment
conglomerates that also include production

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.820
companies, the studios that make TV shows
can shop a series around to different networks,

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.880
which then pay a hefty licensing fee for the
right to air it.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.620
Studios may also team up to make shows, particularly
expensive ones like Deadwood.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:45.260
HBO is a division of Warner Media, and the
network co-produced the show with Paramount,

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.660
which held the international rights.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.020
That necessitated a lot of talks and contracts
between Warner and Paramount over splitting

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.180
the profits from Deadwood.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.460
Those companies reached an impasse about their
arrangement before a fourth season of Deadwood

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:01.170
could go into production, and so both parties
walked away from the table, in effect canceling

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:01.780
the show.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:08.260
"And here's my counteroffer to your counteroffer:
Go f--- yourself."

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.260
That may not be as exciting as a standoff
in the thoroughfare, but it's the reality.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:19.200
Then-HBO chief executive and chairman Chris
Albrecht told the Chicago Tribune,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.860
"Not having a fourth season of Deadwood is
not the result anyone wanted."

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:28.460
By that time, the possibility had disappeared
like a dead miner's body into Mr. Wu's pigpen.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.720
But the network really did fight to keep Deadwood
alive.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:35.720
HBO wanted creator David Milch to concentrate
his efforts on his next show for the network,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:41.100
the surreal, spiritual and short-lived surfing
drama John from Cincinnati, so executives

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.720
offered him a six-episode final season of
Deadwood, as opposed to the traditional 12.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:46.800
He turned it down.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:47.720
Milch said:

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.900
"It seemed to me that some sort of partial
order for the show would make it impossible

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.409
to do anything but superimpose all sort(s)
of interpretations that would deprive it of

00:01:56.409 --> 00:01:57.300
its own emotion."

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:02.620
Then, just days before Deadwood's third and
final season premiered in 2006, HBO announced

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.860
that those episodes would not be the end of
the line, as the network had accepted Milch's

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.760
pitch to wrap up the series with a pair of
two-hour specials.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.400
Network spokesperson Nancy Lesser said,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.900
"He found a way to bring closure to the narrative
and remain true to the themes of the material

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.440
and spirit of the characters."

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.980
Those two specials ended up as just one movie,
released 13 years later.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.980
Deadwood wasn't just canceled because the
people who write the checks had a fight about

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:28.480
money.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.420
It also earned a de facto cancellation because
of some passive-aggressive behavior by HBO.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:37.719
According to TV Series Finale, Deadwood wasn't
"officially" canceled at all.

00:02:37.719 --> 00:02:40.180
The cast's so-called options simply weren't
picked up.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.129
An option is a clause in an actor's contract
that gives a show's production company the

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:48.240
chance to sign the performer on for more episodes
at the end of the contract's term.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:52.380
In the case of Deadwood, actors' contracts
were valid for three seasons, with an option

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.020
for a fourth.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.660
This binds an actor to a show, preventing
them from leaving to make other TV shows or

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.040
movies, thus saving writers from the task
of explaining character absences.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.620
But the fourth-season option for Deadwood's
stars wasn't exercised.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.930
The actors were essentially released from
their obligation to Deadwood, meaning they

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.919
were available to go do other things.

00:03:10.919 --> 00:03:15.370
HBO and the show's producers essentially said,
"you're no longer needed," which is just another

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.680
of saying, "show's over, go home."

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.060
"No deceit too prolonged.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.640
No errand too demeaning.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.400
Get outta here!

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:24.600
No rebuke too vile!"

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.380
Television is a numbers game.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.200
By and large, the shows that bring in the
most overall viewers are the ones that get

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.469
a rubber-stamp renewal season after season.

00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:39.079
HBO is an ad-free network and operates on
a paid subscription model, but it still has

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.680
to take ratings into effect.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.540
If it airs shows that not enough people are
watching, then it's a waste of the company's

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.040
money.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.020
It's also not going to bring in many more
people willing to pay an extra $10 or $20

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.440
a month on their cable bill.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.040
This was the problem with Deadwood.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.400
Despite the critical praise and awards attention
from the Emmys and Golden Globes, it never

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.239
exactly pulled in Game of Thrones-level numbers.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:05.300
The first season of Deadwood averaged a respectable
4.5 million viewers, but only about 2.4 million

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.160
viewers cared enough about Al Swearengen and
Seth Bullock to tune in for season two.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.240
A downward ratings trajectory made an early
cancellation inevitable.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.920
Every episode of Deadwood was densely packed
with inscrutable characters and complicated

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:20.440
dialogue.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.960
"To inform within decency's limits.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.280
We've had this discussion before."

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.080
"Citizens better die pustulatin' than touch
indecent ink."

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:32.880
Deadwood was already a difficult show to make,
even before creator David Milch's tendency

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:34.460
toward last-minute rewrites.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:39.039
According to IndieWire, actor Timothy Olyphant
told the audience at Film Independent Presents'

00:04:39.039 --> 00:04:42.909
"An Evening with Deadwood" about the time
Milch came to him in his trailer to tell him

00:04:42.909 --> 00:04:47.210
that he'd just decided to kill off Bullock's
stepson, which required a complete reworking

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:47.820
of the show.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.760
Olyphant joked:

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.220
"The season became about that kid dying.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.229
[The decision] happened at lunch."

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:57.599
According to Vulture, Milch was a consummate
perfectionist who hung out on the Deadwood

00:04:57.599 --> 00:05:02.479
set, barking out dialogue suggestions from
off-camera and ordering scenes reshot, even

00:05:02.479 --> 00:05:04.740
if he wasn't the credited writer or director.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:09.360
Ian McShane, who played Swearengen, remembers
rewrites coming in so late that the pages

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.180
were still warm from the copy machine.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.380
More takes means more money, and Deadwood
wasn't a cheap show to begin with.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:20.400
Period-appropriate sets and costumes, horses,
wagons, animal handlers, and a large regular

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:26.580
cast all contributed to a budget of around
$4.5 million per episode at its most expensive.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.710
It must have been hard for HBO to justify
keeping Deadwood around when it didn't bring

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:31.700
in huge ratings.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.520
Long before the era of Peak TV brought high-quality
content to most every cable network and streaming

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.430
service, HBO held itself up as the home of
prestige television, bearing the famous slogan,

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:46.120
"It's not TV, it's HBO."

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:50.180
The premium channel carefully selects what
handful of shows it will air at any given

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.090
time, and while it tries to offer a little
something for everyone, it can often find

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.760
itself with a certain "brand."

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:01.400
In the mid-2000s, the flavor of HBO was solidly
hip, envelope-pushing shows set in the present

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:01.900
day.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.319
A sampling of its lineup at the time included
Curb Your Enthusiasm, The Comeback, Extras,

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:08.560
The Sopranos, and Big Love.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:14.200
Among those shows about cranky TV writers,
deluded actresses, cranky British actors,

00:06:14.219 --> 00:06:19.560
depressed mob bosses, and plural marriage
sat Deadwood, an often brutal, slow-moving

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.650
Western where the characters talked like they
were doing Shakespeare with more profanity.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:28.160
It was an anomaly on HBO's schedule, and it
was simply too weird to live.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.360
"Always superfluous, bloodshed!

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:32.720
The deeper damage is best."

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.199
While Deadwood was never a ratings smash,
casting directors and filmmakers were among

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:43.980
those aware of the show's undeniably talented
ensemble cast.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:48.880
By the show's third season in 2006, many Deadwood
performers were already looking to move on

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:50.770
to other roles and other challenges.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:55.419
Ian McShane had roles in a number of films
lined up, including voice work in The Golden

00:06:55.419 --> 00:07:00.539
Compass and Kung Fu Panda, and live action
parts in Hot Rod and The Seeker: The Dark

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:01.140
is Rising.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.699
Molly Parker showed up in Hollywoodland and
The Wicker Man, along with another period

00:07:05.699 --> 00:07:08.860
series, the '70s-set CBS drama Swingtown.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.220
Timothy Olyphant jumped right into Live Free
or Die Hard and Hitman.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:17.180
For many parties, Deadwood's cancellation
came at the right time in their careers.

